course: dictionary
lesson: 8777
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word shaft is a good example. «Mines are shafts and tunnels dug for coal, metals or salt.» Here a shaft is a long, narrow hole that goes straight down into the ground.
explain: The same word can mean something very different. «A shaft of sunlight shines into a dark room.» Here a shaft is a narrow beam of light coming through a gap, not a hole at all. The words around the shaft, sunlight and dark room, are the clues.
explain: Machines use shafts too. In «the pistons turn a shaft that turns the wheels», a shaft is a rod that turns and passes on movement. To pick the right meaning, read the whole sentence, then try each meaning in place of the word and see which one fits.
fact: A shaft is a long, narrow hole that goes straight down or up, such as a mine shaft or a lift shaft.
fact: A shaft is also the long, thin, straight part of a tool, arrow, spear or golf club.
fact: A shaft of light is a narrow beam of light shining through a gap.
fact: In machines, a shaft is a rod that turns and passes on movement, like the shaft in a car.
fact: Safety: never go near old mine shafts or open lift shafts.
exercise: «Mines are shafts and tunnels dug for coal, metals or salt.» What does shaft mean here? | A beam of light | A rod that turns in a machine | *A long, narrow hole going straight down | The thin part of an arrow
exercise: «A shaft of sunlight shines into a dark room.» What does shaft mean here? | A long, narrow hole going down into the ground | A turning rod in a car | The straight part of a golf club | *A narrow beam of light coming through a gap
exercise: «The pistons turn a shaft that turns the wheels.» What does shaft mean here? | A long, narrow hole for mining | A narrow beam of light | The thin part of a spear | *A rod that turns and passes on movement
test: The archer checked the shaft of the arrow for cracks before the contest. What does shaft mean here? | A long, narrow hole going down | *The long, thin, straight part of an arrow | A narrow beam of light | A turning rod in a machine
test: Dad said, «Never go near an open lift shaft.» What does shaft mean here? | A rod that turns in a machine | *A long, narrow space that goes straight up and down | A beam of light through a gap
exam: «A shaft of light fell across the floor.» Which meaning fits best, and why? | A turning rod, because the light is moving | The thin part of an arrow, because the light is thin | *A narrow beam of light, because the light shines through a gap in a straight line | A long, narrow hole going straight down, because light falls into holes
